  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nHigh clouds from the large low pressure system impacting the\nEast Coast continue to spread northward over northwest \nWisconsin this morning, while low stratus is spreading westward \noff Western Lake Superior. The high clouds should prevent\nwidespread dense fog from developing, but will need to keep an\neye on observations along and west of US-53 in northwest WI over\nto the I-35 corridor, where there is less cirrus coverage. If\ndense fog does develop, temperatures are currently in the 20s,\nand freezing fog could lead to slick spots. \n\nLooking to the west, a cold front is moving through North\nDakota, where a north to south oriented band of light rain is\npushing eastward. This band is expected to arrive in north\ncentral MN around mid day. With temperatures starting out rather\nchilly, the area should be above freezing when the band of light\nrain arrives. Surface dewpoints are expected to mix out into \nthe teens or single digits ahead of the front, with forecast \nsoundings indicating that a surface dry layer lingers as \nprofiles saturate above 900mb.\n\nAs the band of light rain arrives, cold air will be pushing \neastward quickly, behind the front. That coupled with \ntemperatures falling at sunset, a wintry mix is possible by late\nafternoon/early evening. A significant warm nose, leading to \nquite the inversion around 875mb, is a good set up for light \nfreezing rain or drizzle. Regardless of precipitation type, \noverall moisture with this system is very limited, which will\nlimit accumulation of any precipitation. \n\nThe best chance for any wintry accumulations will be over the \nArrowhead and east of US-53 across northwest WI, given the \ntiming of the band of light precipitation and surface \ntemperatures cooling below freezing. Dewpoints will also be \nslightly higher in these areas, and the surface dry layer might \nnot be as deep to overcome. Even then, accumulations of rain or\nfreezing rain will be limited to a hundredth or two. \n\nHeading into Saturday and Sunday, the low pressure system in \nOntario will continue to sit and spin just north of the Canadian\nborder. Ongoing cold air advection paired with small amounts of\nmoisture will lead to additional chances for a light wintry mix\nthroughout the weekend. Precipitation type will depend largely \non the time of day, with rain or a wintry mix favored during the\nwarmer daytime hours and light snow at night. Overall amounts \nwill remain very light with minimal travel impacts expected \nacross the area. Cold air moving over Lake Superior will also \ngenerate lake effect snow showers, though most of that snow will\nfall across the Upper Peninsula of Michigan. Behind the system,\nblustery west to northwest winds will develop Saturday into \nSunday, creating breezy conditions over the Northland.\n\nBy early next week, the upper-level pattern shifts to a quasi-zonal\nflow across the Upper Midwest, allowing a train of weak shortwaves to\nmove through the Northland on Monday and Tuesday. Monday looks mostly\nquiet before hit-or-miss chances for light wintry precipitation\nreturn Monday night into Tuesday. Moisture remains limited with these\npassing disturbances, keeping any accumulations light and preventing\nsignificant travel disruptions.\n\nThe low-impact pattern persists into Wednesday as another weak wave\npasses across the region, bringing additional slight chances for\nlight rain and snow. No large storm systems are signaled in model\nguidance through the middle of next week, and temperatures across the\nNorthland are expected to hover near to slightly above seasonal\naverages.",
